        "SIGNALMENT": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "SIGNALMENT",
          "canonical_heading": "SIGNALMENT",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Cyclic hematopoiesis in the collie breed is present only in the color-dilute pups. The color dilution and bone marrow disorder are inherited as an autosomal recessive trait. This condition has also been reported in crossbred collie/beagle pups.",
              "pdf_page": 436,
              "printed_page": 354,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Clinical signs occur as early as 1–2 weeks of age and are always apparent by 4–6 weeks of age. Affected dogs rarely live beyond 2–3 years of age.",
              "pdf_page": 436,
              "printed_page": 354,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "An apparently similar disease was reported in normal-colored pups in two border collie litters in the UK. Cyclic hematopoiesis has also been reported in Pomeranians, Cocker Spaniels, and a Basset Hound, but the disease is not well characterized in these breeds.",
              "pdf_page": 436,
              "printed_page": 354,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Cyclic hematopoiesis has been observed in two cats with feline leukemia virus (FeLV) infection.",
              "pdf_page": 436,
              "printed_page": 354,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
